From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 08:02:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windows.web: implement Windows.Web.Http.HttpMethod and
 HttpClient.

The next two classes Office's React Native host asks for once the filter
exists.  HttpMethod is a name and nothing else, so it is implemented outright,
statics and factory both.  HttpClient is the object the rest of the namespace
hangs off: making one, with or without a filter, and reaching the interfaces it
promises is what a caller does before it sends anything, and that works.

The request methods need HttpRequestMessage, HttpResponseMessage and the
asynchronous operations that carry them, none of which are here yet, so they
report that.  Handing back an operation that never completes would be worse
than failing: the caller waits with nothing to blame.

IIDs are from the published metadata, cross-checked against Office's own
binary.  IHttpMethod is in the metadata but not in react-native-win32.dll,
which only reaches HttpMethod through its statics and factory -- both of which
are in the binary, and both are implemented.

tools/httpfilterprobe grew sections for the two: the seven known methods come
back with the right names, a custom name round-trips, an empty one is refused,
HttpClient activates both ways and refuses a null filter, and every interface
each class promises is reachable.  41 of 41 checks pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 610865660fa5d2158f076ea5c98cb9c3c101c080)

